      "seed": 351911427,
      "version": 20,
      "versionNonce": 1408799459,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793532701059,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "triggers / actions",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "triggers / actions",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "gmPPcTIMx7dmNz2ExJp9a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 372,
      "y": 1718,
      "width": 1235.8054545454538,
      "height": 1710.886363636364,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ac",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 607039885,
      "version": 317,
      "versionNonce": 1270572749,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793533146439,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Flow 1: Command Execution Flow (User Taps \"Turn On\")\n    1. User Action: The user taps the switch on their mobile app.\n    \n    2. API Gateway & Auth: The app issues an HTTPS/REST request: POST /v1/devices/{deviceId}/commands with body:\n        json\n        {\n          \"commands\": [\n            { \"component\": \"main\", \"capability\": \"switch\", \"command\": \"on\", \"arguments\": [] }\n          ]\n        }\n    The API gateway validates the user's JWT/OAuth token and checks location permissions.\n    \n    3. Capability Validation: The platform verifies against the capability schema that switch supports the command on(). Invalid\ncommands are rejected immediately.\n    \n    4. Routing Decision (Local vs. Cloud):\n        i. Local Route (Hub-connected Zigbee, Z-Wave, Matter, LAN): The cloud routes the command via a persistent connection\nto the local SmartThings Hub. The hub's Edge Driver (written in Lua) translates the SmartThings command into protocol-\nspecific frames (e.g., Matter OnOff Cluster 0x0006, Zigbee ZCL On).\n        ii. Cloud-to-Cloud Route (e.g., Philips Hue cloud, Tuya): The command is dispatched to the manufacturer's OAuth\nwebhook / Cloud Connector.\n    Direct Connected Route: Routed through the Cloud IoT Gateway (CoAP over TCP / MQTT).\n    5. Physical Execution: The physical actuator turns on the relay.\n\n\n\n\nFlow 2: Telemetry & State Update Flow (Device → App & Cloud)\n    1. Physical Trigger: A sensor trips (e.g., motion detected or switch pressed manually).\n    \n    2. Event Parsing:\n        i. If on a Hub, the Edge Driver decodes the raw radio packet and calls\ndevice:emit_event(capabilities.motionSensor.motion.active()).\n        ii. The Hub/Gateway forwards the standardized payload to the cloud ingestion broker (Kafka topic: device.telemetry).\n    \n    3. State Ingestion & Normalization:\n         i. Hot Cache: The state is updated in Redis (HSET device:{id}:state switch \"on\").\n        ii. Time-Series Persistence: Stored asynchronously in Cassandra / S3 for analytics, history graphs, and energy rollups.\n    \n    4. Rules & Automations Trigger:\n        i. The Rules Engine consumes the event from Kafka.\n        ii. It checks if any active Rules, Scenes, or Security monitors depend on motion == active or switch == on.\n        iii. If conditions are met, it triggers secondary actions (e.g., turn on hallway light).\n    \n    5. Push to Client:\n        i. A notification service publishes the state change over a persistent WebSocket or Server-Sent Events (SSE) channel\nconnected to the user's mobile app.\n        ii. The app UI flips the tile state to \"On\" in real time without polling.\n\n\nFlow 3: Presentation & UI Rendering Flow (Metadata to Screen)\n\n    How does the app know how to draw a device it has never seen before?\n    \n    1. The app queries GET /v1/devices/{deviceId}.\n    \n    2. The response contains the device's deviceProfileId, which includes metadata: vid: \"generic-dimmer\" and mnmn:\n\"SmartThingsCommunity\".\n    \n    3. Presentation Lookup:\n        i. The app checks its local SQLite cache for (vid, mnmn).\n        i. If missing, it fetches the UI schema from GET /v1/presentation?vid=...&mnmn=....\n    \n    4. Layout Assembly: The presentation specifies:\n        i. Dashboard Tile: Which capability attribute shows primary status (e.g., switch.state), which capability provides the\nquick action button.\n        ii. Detail View: Ordered sections (e.g., Section 1 = On/Off toggle; Section 2 = Brightness slider; Section 3 = Power\nconsumption chart).\n        iii. Automation View: Which capabilities are selectable as \"If\" triggers and \"Then\" actions.",
      "fontSize": 19.83636363636364,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Flow 1: Command Execution Flow (User Taps \"Turn On\")\n    1. User Action: The user taps the switch on their mobile app.\n    \n    2. API Gateway & Auth: The app issues an HTTPS/REST request: POST /v1/devices/{deviceId}/commands with body:\n        json\n        {\n          \"commands\": [\n            { \"component\": \"main\", \"capability\": \"switch\", \"command\": \"on\", \"arguments\": [] }\n          ]\n        }\n    The API gateway validates the user's JWT/OAuth token and checks location permissions.\n    \n    3. Capability Validation: The platform verifies against the capability schema that switch supports the command on(). Invalid commands are rejected immediately.\n    \n    4. Routing Decision (Local vs. Cloud):\n        i. Local Route (Hub-connected Zigbee, Z-Wave, Matter, LAN): The cloud routes the command via a persistent connection to the local SmartThings Hub. The hub's Edge Driver (written in Lua) translates the SmartThings command into protocol-specific frames (e.g., Matter OnOff Cluster 0x0006, Zigbee ZCL On).\n        ii. Cloud-to-Cloud Route (e.g., Philips Hue cloud, Tuya): The command is dispatched to the manufacturer's OAuth webhook / Cloud Connector.\n    Direct Connected Route: Routed through the Cloud IoT Gateway (CoAP over TCP / MQTT).\n    5. Physical Execution: The physical actuator turns on the relay.\n\n\n\n\nFlow 2: Telemetry & State Update Flow (Device → App & Cloud)\n    1. Physical Trigger: A sensor trips (e.g., motion detected or switch pressed manually).\n    \n    2. Event Parsing:\n        i. If on a Hub, the Edge Driver decodes the raw radio packet and calls device:emit_event(capabilities.motionSensor.motion.active()).\n        ii. The Hub/Gateway forwards the standardized payload to the cloud ingestion broker (Kafka topic: device.telemetry).\n    \n    3. State Ingestion & Normalization:\n         i. Hot Cache: The state is updated in Redis (HSET device:{id}:state switch \"on\").\n        ii. Time-Series Persistence: Stored asynchronously in Cassandra / S3 for analytics, history graphs, and energy rollups.\n    \n    4. Rules & Automations Trigger:\n        i. The Rules Engine consumes the event from Kafka.\n        ii. It checks if any active Rules, Scenes, or Security monitors depend on motion == active or switch == on.\n        iii. If conditions are met, it triggers secondary actions (e.g., turn on hallway light).\n    \n    5. Push to Client:\n        i. A notification service publishes the state change over a persistent WebSocket or Server-Sent Events (SSE) channel connected to the user's mobile app.\n        ii. The app UI flips the tile state to \"On\" in real time without polling.\n\n\nFlow 3: Presentation & UI Rendering Flow (Metadata to Screen)\n\n    How does the app know how to draw a device it has never seen before?\n    \n    1. The app queries GET /v1/devices/{deviceId}.\n    \n    2. The response contains the device's deviceProfileId, which includes metadata: vid: \"generic-dimmer\" and mnmn: \"SmartThingsCommunity\".\n    \n    3. Presentation Lookup:\n        i. The app checks its local SQLite cache for (vid, mnmn).\n        i. If missing, it fetches the UI schema from GET /v1/presentation?vid=...&mnmn=....\n    \n    4. Layout Assembly: The presentation specifies:\n        i. Dashboard Tile: Which capability attribute shows primary status (e.g., switch.state), which capability provides the quick action button.\n        ii. Detail View: Ordered sections (e.g., Section 1 = On/Off toggle; Section 2 = Brightness slider; Section 3 = Power consumption chart).\n        iii. Automation View: Which capabilities are selectable as \"If\" triggers and \"Then\" actions.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
